O|                                    The Father  Thing                               
O|
G,I|               ON CHARLIE COTRELL                                                   
N,I|               A typical 11-year-old. But with dried blood on his face.             
N|               Talking to us -- or at least that is what it seems.                  
O|
M|               In actuality, he is recording a message for THE OTHERS. But          
M|               we will not realize that until the end of our story.                 
O|
N|               Charlie Cotrell is an eloquent kid:                                  
O|
C|                                   CHARLIE                                          
U|                         My father’s not a dick.                                    
O|
N|               Charlie struggles.                                                   
C,E|                                   CHARLIE (CONT’D)                                 
U|                         I mean he was always there. Last                           
U|                         month he had to go to New York for                         
U|                         a conference. But he came back two                         
U|                         days early because he didn’t want                          
U|                         to miss the playoffs--                                     
O|
T|                                                              SMASH TO:             
O|
S,M|               EXT. BASEBALL FIELD - FLASHBACK - ONE MONTH AGO - DAY                
N|               Charlie is at the plate.                                             
O|
N,I|               The PITCHER, another 11-year-old (but God if he doesn’t look         
N,I|               like a 23-year-old ringer from Panama with a shitty mustache)        
N|               fingers the ball in his left hand, stares from the mound,            
N|               winds up and lets loose a 70 mph fastball.                           
O|
N|               Charlie lifts the bat from his shoulders and:                        
O|
N|               CRACK                                                                
O|
N|               Charlie makes contact, the ball rocketing down the left field        
N|               line just past the outstretched glove of the THIRD BASEMAN.          
N|               The ball gains a little altitude and then drops into the             
N|               corner of the Pony League field.                                     
O|
N,G|               ON CHARLIE                                                           
O|
N|               He rounds first heading to second, keeps going, rounds third,        
N|               and tears up the base path toward home plate.                        
O|
O|
O|                                                                      2.            
O|
N|               The CATCHER throws off his mask and steps forward as it all          
N,G|               SLOWS DOWN 113-fold.                                                 
N|               Charlie looks up. The SOUND drops away. There are a HUNDRED          
N|               YELLING PEOPLE filling the rickety stands, but they are              
N,G|               silent, out of focus, and the only person Charlie can SEE is         
N|               his FATHER.                                                          
O|
N|               Charlie’s Father rises to his feet.                                  
O|
N,G|               And then everything SPEEDS UP and is over in a nanosecond.           
O|
N|               A lot of screaming. Charlie hurtling.  The Catcher sneering.         
N|               Charlie sliding on his stomach. The sound of the ball in the         
N|               Catcher’s mitt. A huge dust cloud around home plate. The             
N|               tag.  And after an eternity:                                         
C|                                   HOME PLATE UMPIRE                                
U|                         S-s-safe!                                                  
O|
N|               Charlie looks up from the dirt, SEES:                                
O|
N|               THE FATHER                                                           
O|
N|               A big ass smile on his face.                                         
O|
S|               EXT. BASEBALL FIELD - LATER                                          
O|
N|               The light has fallen over the deserted diamond. Hanging over         
N|               the field is the MOON. It’s full.  Huge. Surreal.                    
O|
N|               A METALLIC ELECTRIC PITCHING MACHINE is on the pitching              
N|               mound.  Resting on the hood of the machine is a baseball cap.        
N|               It looks like a little robot. Next to the machine, The               
N|               Father, loading baseballs from a plastic bucket.                     
O|
N|               Charlie swings at ball after ball. Determined. Gonna work            
N|               it to death.                                                         
C|                                   THE FATHER                                       
U|                         Stay inside the ball. Hit through                          
U|                         it.                                                        
O|
N|               Charlie takes a deep cut, hits a rocket that smacks the              
N|               machine, knocking off the cap.                                       
O|
T|                                                              SMASH TO:             
O|
S|               CHARLIE COTRELL - IN FRONT OF HIS COMPUTER - RESUME                  
O|
N|               Still at it, cold-blue light from the computer illuminating          
N|               his face.                                                            
O|
O|
O|                                                                      3.            
O|
C|                                   CHARLIE                                          
U|                         I think my father would be proud of                        
U|                         me.                                                        
T,G|                                                           BLACK, THEN:             
